# What is known about particular cellular modules, as data.
#
# Decision 0043's shape: a quirk is a table entry somebody can read, diff and
# contribute without touching code, keyed on the USB id. A quirk expressed as a
# branch grows a second branch; a quirk expressed as a row does not.
#
# ## THIS TABLE EXPLAINS. IT DOES NOT DECIDE.
#
# The helpers behave correctly on a module that is not listed here, and that is
# a rule rather than an aspiration: `netcfgd-modem-at` waits for the module to
# dial and dials itself if it has not, which is right whether or not the module
# autoconnects. A table that *decided* would make every unlisted module behave
# worse -- and the unlisted module is the one somebody is holding when it
# fails.
#
# So what this is for is answering "why is this module like that", picking a
# sensible default port, and saying which helper applies at all.
#
# ## EVERY ROW IS MEASURED, AND SAYS ON WHAT
#
# A row nobody has run is a guess with a citation, which is worse than an empty
# table -- somebody will trust it. If you add one, add it from a module you
# have in front of you, and say what you read it off.
#
# ## FORMAT
#
#   vid:pid  key=value key=value ...
#
#   data=       ecm | mbim | qmi | rndis  -- the data path the firmware offers
#   helper=     at | mbim                 -- which helper can drive it
#   at=         N                         -- ttyUSB index that answers AT and
#                                            that a modem manager does not hold
#   autoconnect=yes | no                  -- the module dials its own context
#                                            on registration
#   note=       free text, no spaces around `=`
#
# `data=ecm` with `helper=mbim` would be a contradiction: a module offering only
# ECM presents no /dev/cdc-wdm for mbimcli to open, which is the whole reason
# the AT helper exists.

# Quectel EG916Q-GL. Measured 2026-07-28 and after, on an i.MX93 board with the
# module on a daughtercard: `+QCFG: "usbnet",1` (ECM; the firmware offers only
# 1 and 3, so no MBIM and no QMI exist to select), `+QCFG: "qcautoconnect",1`,
# `+QCFG: "nat",0`. Composition is four `option` ports plus `cdc_ether`.
#
# `at=3` because a running ModemManager held `ttyUSB2` and left `ttyUSB0`,
# `ttyUSB1` and `ttyUSB3` free -- checked by looking at what had the ports open
# rather than assumed from the port order. On a machine with no modem manager,
# 2 answers as well. `ttyUSB0` is the diagnostic port and answers binary;
# `ttyUSB1` answers nothing at all.
#
# **ModemManager takes both AT ports once the modem registers** -- `ttyUSB2`
# only while unregistered, and `ttyUSB3` as well afterwards, which is precisely
# when the PDP context first has an APN and resolvers to read. Measured
# 2026-09-11 on an i.MX91. Where MM is running, a udev rule setting
# `ID_MM_PORT_IGNORE=1` on USB interface 3 is what keeps the port available;
# MM does not need it and drove the module from `ttyUSB2` alone throughout.
# Without that rule, `AT+CGCONTRDP` becomes unreadable exactly when it matters,
# which the AT helper reports as "not known" rather than filling in.
#
# `nat=0` is why the host takes a DHCP lease: the module bridges the operator's
# address rather than handing out a private one, so the ECM interface gets the
# real address and `config = "dhcp"` is all netcfgd needs.
#
# **Its DHCP also offers resolvers, and they do not answer.** `192.168.10.3`
# and `.4`, on the module's own pre-attach subnet; neither responds to a query.
# The working pair is in the PDP context and comes out of `AT+CGCONTRDP`. An
# interface here should therefore carry no `dns { }` block, which is how
# netcfgd says "address from the lease, nameservers not".
2c7c:6007  data=ecm helper=at at=3 autoconnect=yes note=EG916Q-GL,firmware_EG916QGLLGR01A05M04

# ## WHAT THIS FIRMWARE REFUSES OR ANSWERS VACUOUSLY
#
# All measured on the module above, 2026-08-27 and 2026-09-11. Each is here
# because it reads as a fact about the SIM or the radio and is a fact about the
# firmware, which is the most expensive kind of wrong answer.
#
# **`AT+CRSM` is refused** -- `CME ERROR: 3` for every file, `EF_ICCID`
# included, and that last one is the control: a file the card certainly has,
# refused the same way, proves it is the command rather than the file. SIM file
# access has to go through `AT+CCHO` and `AT+CGLA`, and `CCHO` wants a
# **partial** AID -- `A0000000871002` opens a channel where the full 16-byte
# padded USIM AID fails.
#
# **`AT+QSIMSTAT?` is a vacuous pass.** With `+QSIMDET: 0,0` the module reports
# "inserted" unconditionally; it said so with the socket empty. Anything built
# on it while detection is off is reading a constant, not a card.
#
# **There is no hot-plug detection** (`+QSIMDET: 0,0`), so a card fitted after
# boot is invisible until the modem is reset. That is why a SIM change is a
# reset and not a re-read.
#
# **An ISD-R probe cannot tell a socket from an eUICC.** `AT+CCHO` on the ISD-R
# AID reports absent at both mux positions, including one demonstrably holding
# an eUICC -- the module blocks it. The ICCID is the only discriminator, which
# is what `netcfgd-modem-at iccid` is for.
#
# **`AT+CFUN=1,1` is not a safe reset on every board.** On one i.MX93 carrier it
# rebooted the whole SoC -- uptime back to minutes, `/tmp` cleared -- and the
# same command on an i.MX91 with the same module was harmless. It is the
# carrier rather than the module, which is exactly why no helper here sends it:
# resetting a modem is board enablement, and on a board with a reset line it is
# that line's job.
#
# ## WHAT LOOKS LIKE A SIM FAULT AND IS NOT
#
# **A socket that cannot read is indistinguishable from an empty socket.**
# Nothing in the AT interface separates them: a card that registers home in one
# board read as absent in another's socket, `AT+CPIN?` giving `CME ERROR: 10`,
# while the same runs read that board's eUICC through the same mux. Mux and
# module were both fine; it was the socket's path on that board.
#
# **An eUICC with no enabled profile presents as no SIM.** That is not a fault
# and no APN will change it.
#
# **`ESM cause 33` is the subscription, not the APN.**
# `REQUESTED_SERVICE_OPTION_NOT_SUBSCRIBED`: the profile is live enough to
# attempt registration and has no data entitlement until it is activated.
# Measured against a shipped bootstrap profile, an empty APN and one operator
# APN both gave cause 29 while two others gave 33 -- so a sweep of APNs against
# a profile in this state reads as "every APN is wrong" and means none of them
# was the problem. `AT+CEER` is what the AT helper prints on a failed attach
# for this reason.
